{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>And you shall make.</b> Our ancestors said that the form of the cherubim is like two youths, and they explained כרוביא in Aramaic, and the kaf is servile. Their statement is correct that they were like the form of children, if it is tradition. And he made the word masculine, like the word חנוכה, for the kaf of כרוב is a root letter. And the proof is והיו ההכרובים (Exodus 20 [20:20]) — for the heh is the article and is not for the feminine, like the heh of הכמכת מכהו הכהו (Isaiah 27:7). And when I searched the word כרובים, behold it is a form, like אַתְּ כְּרוּב מִמְשַׁח הַסֹּכֵךְ (Ezekiel 28:14). Ezekiel said that he saw HaShem's face for the creature, and one of them was the face of an ox, and in another place, concerning the creature itself: תַּחַת שׁוֹר פְּנֵי הַכְּרוּב (ibid. 10:14). And the meaning: the form that he mentioned the first time and said in the last instance is the creature that I saw beneath the Elohim of Israel at the river Chebar, and I know that they were כרובים (ibid. v. 20). And he called all four כרובים — behold, the kaf is a root letter." "<b>Hammered.</b> Like שָׁוָה, and similar to it: וּתַחַת מַעֲשֵׂה מִקְשָׁה (Isaiah 3:24). And the meaning of מַעֲשֵׂה is like לֹא־עָשָׂה אֶת־שְׂפָמוֹ (2 Samuel 19:25). And the meaning: because each cherub, equal [in form], was distanced from the edge of the cover."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {0 "The verse reference 'פ׳ כ' in the first proof is resolved as Exodus 20:20 based on 'והיו ההכרובים' matching the end of that verse. Ibn Ezra appears to cite an unusual verse; the logic of the argument from the definite article is preserved as written."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:38:25.160146141Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}